#!/usr/bin/env python3
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  apex-installer-gui — graphical front end for the APEX-OS installer
#
#  THIS IS A FRONT END ONLY. It collects answers and hands them to
#  `apex-install --headless`, which is the install engine: every disk guard,
#  LVM/LUKS refusal, ESP check, bootc invocation, account creation, SELinux
#  relabel and Secure Boot step lives there, not here. There is exactly one
#  install engine and this is not it.
#
#  It is also the ONLY front end. The text-mode installer that used to sit
#  behind this as a fallback has been deleted: on real hardware the GUI failed
#  to start for reasons that were all fixable image bugs, and users were
#  stranded in a text UI they were never meant to see. Everything the text UI
#  could collect, this must be able to collect — including the Secure Boot MOK
#  password — or the capability simply no longer exists.
#
#  Design (see docs/branding.md — colours are the established APEX tokens, not
#  invented here): industrial/technical instrument. Mono for anything the
#  machine said (device paths, sizes, serials); humanist sans for prose. The
#  accent follows the EDITION being installed — chartreuse for Daily, gold for
#  Gaming — so the installer looks like the thing it is about to install.
#
#  The memorable screen is Confirm: a stark, honest inventory of what dies and
#  what survives. That screen is why this exists — whiptail silently CLIPPED it
#  when a disk had five or more partitions, hiding "ALL DATA WILL BE LOST"
#  exactly when there was data to lose. A scrolling GTK list cannot do that.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
